# NASA Power of 10 — Overview

Ten rules for safety-critical code, distilled by Gerard Holzmann at JPL (Holzmann 2006). Apply in any language whose runtime semantics permit them. Each rule has its own skill — invoke it for full guidance, examples, and per-language remediation.

## The Ten Rules

| # | Rule | Severity | Skill |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Restrict control flow to simple constructs | blocker | `pow10-rule-01-control-flow` |
| 2 | Give all loops a fixed upper bound | blocker | `pow10-rule-02-bounded-loops` |
| 3 | No dynamic memory allocation after initialization | blocker | `pow10-rule-03-no-dynamic-memory` |
| 4 | Keep functions short (~60 lines max) | high | `pow10-rule-04-short-functions` |
| 5 | At least 2 runtime assertions per function on average | high | `pow10-rule-05-assertion-density` |
| 6 | Declare data at smallest possible scope | medium | `pow10-rule-06-minimum-scope` |
| 7 | Check return values; validate parameters | blocker | `pow10-rule-07-check-return-values` |
| 8 | Limit preprocessor to includes + simple macros | high | `pow10-rule-08-limited-preprocessor` |
| 9 | Restrict pointers; no function pointers | blocker | `pow10-rule-09-restrict-pointers` |
| 10 | All warnings on; treat warnings as errors | blocker | `pow10-rule-10-warnings-as-errors` |

## When To Apply

- Embedded firmware, flight software, medical devices, automotive ECUs
- Any code path where a crash or undefined behavior is unrecoverable
- High-assurance subsets of otherwise non-critical codebases (boot, ISR, recovery)

## When NOT To Apply (verbatim)

- Application code with rich runtime support (web apps, scripts, batch jobs)
- Code whose correctness story is "tests + monitoring + restart"

You can still steal individual rules (4, 5, 6, 7, 10 port well anywhere) without committing to the full ten.

## Languages Covered (v0.1)

C, Go, Python, Java, Kotlin. Rule 3 (no heap) and Rule 9 (no function pointers) need careful adaptation outside C — see each rule's per-language section.

## Waiver Convention

When a rule must be broken, leave an inline comment so reviewers and future agents can find it:

```
// pow10: allow rule=N until=YYYY-MM-DD owner=<handle> reason="..."
```

Use the file's native comment syntax (`//`, `#`, `--`). All four fields required. No statefile — waivers live next to the code.

## Reviewing Code Against pow10

Use the `pow10-review` skill or invoke the `pow10-auditor` agent for a structured pass over a diff or file.

## Authoring Convention

Each rule's SKILL.md stays under 200 lines. When a rule's per-language guidance grows past that threshold, split per-language sections into `references/<lang>.md` (C, Go, Python, Java, Kotlin) and load only the relevant language at runtime. See `CONTRIBUTING.md` for the split layout.

## Citations

- Holzmann, G. (2006). "The Power of 10: Rules for Developing Safety-Critical Code." *IEEE Computer* 39(6), 95–97.
